# Internet Culture Hub — Article Creation Workflow

Follow every step below when creating a new article. Do not skip steps.
See `.cursor/rules/content-research.mdc` for research and accuracy requirements.

---

## Step 0 — Research BEFORE writing (mandatory)

See `.cursor/rules/content-research.mdc` for the full research checklist.

Before any code:
1. Identify the entity: What is it? What is it NOT?
2. Confirm the category using the research rule's classification table
3. Write a one-sentence definition — if you cannot, research more first
4. Locate at least one real **public** source (Wikipedia, news, official, Wiktionary) — Know Your Meme is research-only, never list it in `sources`

**Output a brief CONTENT RESEARCH REPORT before writing the article:**

```
CONTENT RESEARCH REPORT
Entity: [name]
Type: [what it is in one sentence]
Category: [meme / slang / event / creator / trend]
What it is NOT: [common misclassification risk]
Origin: [platform, year, community — from verified source]
Sources found: [URLs]
```

---

## Step 1 — Check for duplicates FIRST

Before writing a single line:

1. Search every category folder for the intended slug:

```typescript
// Search every category folder for the intended slug
grep -r 'slug: "your-slug"' lib/content/
```

2. Check near-duplicate concepts (title similarity). After drafting a title, run `npm run validate` and review `TITLE_SIMILARITY` warnings (e.g. Sigma / Sigma Male / Sigma Grindset). Warn-only — human decides merge vs keep distinct.

If the slug exists **anywhere** — in any category — do NOT create a new file.
Tell the user which file already covers the topic and offer to improve it instead.

Also check `lib/content/aliases/registry.ts` — if an alias already maps the phrase to another slug, improve that article instead of creating a parallel entry.

---

## Step 2 — Determine category, type, and folder

| Category | TypeScript type | Folder | Index file |
|---|---|---|---|
| `meme` | `MemeEntry` | `lib/content/memes/` | `lib/content/memes/index.ts` |
| `slang` | `SlangEntry` | `lib/content/slang/` | `lib/content/slang/index.ts` |
| `event` | `EventEntry` | `lib/content/events/` | `lib/content/events/index.ts` |
| `creator` | `CreatorEntry` | `lib/content/creators/` | `lib/content/creators/index.ts` |
| `brainrot` | `BrainrotEntry` | `lib/content/brainrot/` | `lib/content/brainrot/index.ts` |
| `trend` | `BaseEntry` | `lib/content/trends/` | `lib/content/trends/index.ts` |

**`TrendEntry` does not exist. Trend articles use `BaseEntry` directly.**

If an article is trending AND belongs to another category (e.g., a meme that is currently viral), create it in the canonical category folder and import it into `lib/content/trends/index.ts`. Do NOT create a separate stub.

---

## Step 3 — Generate the slug

- Lowercase, hyphens only, no special characters: `my-article-slug`
- Must be unique across ALL categories, not just the target one
- Filename must match slug exactly: `lib/content/memes/my-article-slug.ts`

---

## Step 4 — Assign the next sequential ID

**Always use the CLI** (do not guess):

```bash
npm run next-id meme    # → m42
npm run next-id slang   # → s41
npm run next-id event
npm run next-id creator
npm run next-id trend
npm run next-id brainrot
```

| Category | Format | Example |
|---|---|---|
| meme | `m{N}` | `m42` |
| slang | `s{N}` | `s41` |
| event | `e{N}` | `e14` |
| creator | `cr{N}` | `cr25` |
| trend | `t{N}` | `t16` |
| brainrot | `br{N}` | `br1` |

---

## Step 5 — Create the file using the correct template

Templates live in `lib/content/templates/articleTemplate.ts`.

| Category | Template export |
|---|---|
| meme | `MEME_TEMPLATE` |
| slang | `SLANG_TEMPLATE` |
| event | `EVENT_TEMPLATE` |
| creator | `CREATOR_TEMPLATE` |
| trend | `TREND_TEMPLATE` |

Copy the matching template and fill every field. Required fields by type:

**All entries (`BaseEntry`):**
`id`, `slug`, `title`, `category`, `description`, `imageGradient`, `scores` (`relevance`, `influence`, `cringe`, `brainrot`), `addedAt`, `views`, `trendDirection`

**`MemeEntry` adds (all required):**
`origin`, `meaning`, `timeline`, `examples`, `relatedSlugs`

**`SlangEntry` adds (all required):**
`definition`, `origin`, `usageExamples`, `relatedSlugs`
> `partOfSpeech` does NOT exist on `SlangEntry` — describe it in `definition` instead.

**`EventEntry` adds (all required):**
`impact`, `highlights`, `relatedSlugs`

**`CreatorEntry` adds (optional):**
`careerStart`, `platforms`, `followers`, `notableMoments`

**`BrainrotEntry` adds (optional):**
`loreUniverse`, `targetAgeGroup`, `relatedSlugs`

---

## Step 6 — Register in the category index

Add import and push to the exported array. Example for a new meme:

```typescript
// lib/content/memes/index.ts
import myNewMeme from "./my-new-meme";

export const memes: MemeEntry[] = [
  // existing entries ...
  myNewMeme,
];
```

For trending cross-category entries, import the canonical file into `trends/index.ts`:

```typescript
// lib/content/trends/index.ts — DO NOT create a stub; import from canonical location
import myNewMeme from "../memes/my-new-meme";
```

---

## Step 6b — Aliases (when needed)

If the topic has alternate spellings or common search phrases, add them to the centralized registry (not the article file):

```typescript
// lib/content/aliases/registry.ts
"type-shii": ["type shit", "type shi", "type shii"],
```

## Step 6c — Relationships

Prefer typed cultural edges in `relationships` over random same-category filler.
Keep `relatedSlugs` compatible (editorial shortcut). Fewer high-quality links is better.

Example relationship keys: `memberOf`, `popularized`, `originated`, `inspiredBy`, `relatedSlang`, `relatedEvent`, `sameEra`, `sameFormat`, `community`.

Do **not** invent relationships. Only add edges you can justify from research.

---

## Step 7 — Apply media rules

Full rules: `.cursor/rules/media-architecture.mdc`

Quick reference:

| Category | Media guidance |
|---|---|
| Creator | Featured photo strongly recommended (Wikimedia Commons CC-licensed) |
| Meme | Featured image required if the meme is visually defined |
| Event | Featured image of the defining moment; video if one defined it |
| Slang | Most never needs media — color fallback is correct |
| Trend | Usually needs media — color fallback is correct |

**Never invent or guess URLs.** All AI-suggested media must set `verified: false`.
Humans open the URL, confirm it loads the correct image, then set `verified: true`.

---

## Step 8 — Post-creation verification (mandatory — ALL boxes must pass)

This is the mandatory completion checklist. An article is NOT complete until every item is checked.

### 8a — Run automated checks

```bash
npm run validate          # unified P0 content gates (errors fail the run)
npm run audit:media       # offline media readiness report (warnings only)
npm run audit:media:live  # optional HEAD + YouTube oEmbed (never auto-verifies)
npm run build             # runs validate via prebuild, then TypeScript + Next.js build
```

`npm run validate` must exit 0 (warnings OK). `npm run build` must pass.

### 8b — Media verification checklist

For every MediaItem in the article:

**Featured image**
- [ ] URL returns a real image (not a webpage, not a 404, not a redirect to a generic placeholder)
- [ ] URL uses a reliable host: Wikimedia Commons, YouTube CDN, or official site
- [ ] Hero displays correctly on the article page
- [ ] Card image displays correctly on the category listing page
- [ ] Does NOT use `maxresdefault.jpg` from YouTube (often missing for older videos) — use `hqdefault.jpg` instead
- [ ] Does NOT use Pinterest, Imgur, Gyazo, Twitter CDN (pbs.twimg), Reddit CDN, Facebook CDN, Instagram CDN

**Supporting images**
- [ ] Every URL returns a real image
- [ ] No broken image icons in the gallery
- [ ] Captions/titles display correctly

**Video embeds**
- [ ] Video exists on YouTube (verify via `https://www.youtube.com/oembed?url=VIDEO_URL&format=json` — returns `Not Found` for deleted/private)
- [ ] Embedding is not disabled (oembed `html` field contains iframe)
- [ ] Video plays inside the gallery player
- [ ] Video is the correct content (not a different video with same ID)

**References**
- [ ] URL resolves to the expected page (not a 404)
- [ ] Source title in the article matches the destination page

### 8c — Complete completion gate

An article is only complete when ALL of the following are true:

- [ ] Research complete and documented (CONTENT RESEARCH REPORT output)
- [ ] Sources verified (not invented dates, creators, or statistics)
- [ ] Prose teaches first — answers what / why people cared / why it spread / why remembered / influence (`docs/EDITORIAL_STYLE_GUIDE.md`)
- [ ] Historical context explained (not assumed); concrete examples used; fields read as one story
- [ ] No unearned superlatives or robotic / generic AI filler (`PROSE_STYLE` warnings reviewed)
- [ ] `npm run validate` passes (0 errors)
- [ ] TypeScript compiles without errors (`npm run build` passes)
- [ ] Media verified: all URLs return correct content
- [ ] Hero image works on the article page
- [ ] Gallery images work on the article page
- [ ] Video embeds work on the article page
- [ ] `npm run audit:media` reviewed for new media warnings
- [ ] `npm run build` passes without new errors

Do not report the task complete until every box above can be checked.

---

## Common mistakes — never do these

| ❌ Mistake | ✅ Correct |
|---|---|
| Creating a stub in `trends/` when a canonical article exists | Import canonical entry into `trends/index.ts` |
| Importing `TrendEntry` from `@/types` | Use `BaseEntry` for trend articles |
| Adding `partOfSpeech` to `SlangEntry` | Describe it inside `definition` |
| Setting `verified: true` on AI-suggested URLs | Always set `verified: false`; human verifies |
| Using `imageUrl` or `mediaEmbeds` | Use `media?: MediaItem[]` only |
| Using Wikimedia `/thumb/` CDN paths | Use full-file `upload.wikimedia.org` URLs |
| Duplicate slug across categories | Search first; improve existing article instead |
| Listing facts without teaching why they mattered | Answer the five encyclopedia questions in flowing prose |
| Assuming the reader knows the era/platform | Explain the internet environment in one or two concrete sentences |
| Disconnected `origin` / `impact` blocks | Write transitions so fields read as one story |
